# ADR conventions

## Location and naming

- Write ADRs only under `adr/`.
- Filename: `NNNN-kebab-case-short-title.md` (4-digit, zero-padded). Never renumber.
- Title voice: decision-shaped (`use-postgresql`, `choose-x-over-y`).
- Copy `adr/template.md` → next free `NNNN-….md`. No separate index/README.

```text
# ❌ BAD
docs/architecture-decisions/ADR-001-Choose Speckit.md

# ✅ GOOD
adr/0002-use-postgresql-as-primary-store.md
```

## When to write

- Datastore, broker, language, or major library choice/replacement
- Repo layout / monorepo topology that other work will depend on
- Delivery / consistency semantics (outbox, idempotency, retries)
- Waiving or specializing a constitution default (timeouts, budgets, security shortcuts)
- Any decision you expect to re-debate without written rationale

## Workflow (agents)

1. Draft ADR as **`Proposed`**
2. Present it for human review — **do not implement** dependent code yet
3. On explicit acceptance, set status to **`Accepted`**, then implement
4. Never ship ADR + code in one step without that review gate

## Content rules

- **One decision per file.** Context → Decision → Consequences → Alternatives.
- Status: `Proposed` | `Accepted` | `Deprecated` | `Superseded by NNNN`.
- Once **Accepted**, do not rewrite the decision; supersede with a new ADR and update the old status.
- Prefer links to `docs/` for long design; keep the ADR the “why.”

## Size and when to split

- Target ≤ **500 words** / ~**80 lines** body; hard stop ~**1,000 words** / **150 lines**.
- Split (or extract a design doc) when the draft:
  1. Answers more than one independent question
  2. Exceeds the hard stop after cutting fluff
  3. Needs diagrams, runbooks, or implementation walkthroughs
  4. Replaces an Accepted ADR (write new + mark old superseded)

## Do not

- Bundle “and also we pick Kafka and Redis and …” into one ADR
- Pad with tutorial prose or full schemas
- Edit Accepted decision text in place to flip the choice
- Maintain an `adr/README.md` index
